Z||000000||Will||03-04-2001||06:56 PM||||I was just looking through <A HREF="http://www.mantapart.com" TARGET=_blank>www.mantapart.com</A>  They have a hub carrier and brake kit which uses an aftermarket hub carrier casting to put 11+" brakes on the front of cars like the Sunbird, Grand-Am, etc. How similar are the front hub carriers of these cars to the rear hub carriers of '84-87 Fieros?<BR>They have 3.94 final drives, clutch pack or Torsen LSD's, and close ratio gearsets for the Getrag.<BR>They also have 3.83 and 4.11 final drives, close ratio gearsets, and clutch pack or quaife LSD's for the Isuzu five speed.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000001||Greg Piet||03-04-2001||07:10 PM||||Well it is possible to do a Grand am upgrae on a Fiero so I guess if it fits a grand am it can fit a Fiero as well.  the ogre just went over the factors on the grand am upgrade in the Brake math posting, it would be wise to consult that before any brake upgrades/downgrades||||reg||1
Z||000002||Will||03-04-2001||08:19 PM||||Their brake upgrades replace the whole steering knuckle, and all brake hardware on<BR>86-94 Grand Am, Calais, & Achieva<BR>83-94 Cavalier & Sunbird<BR>95 & Up Grand Am & Achieva<BR>87-94 Beretta & Corsica<P>IOW, NONE of the parts are stock. This has no relation to the Grand Am brake swap as it is usually considered.<P>What I want to know is: how does their replacement knuckle (and thus the mounting points of the stock knuckles of the cars in question) compare to the Fiero/Citation hubcarrier, which is not mentioned on their website?<P>Yes, I have already e-mailed them to ask.<P>The hub carrier question is moot anyway. I already heard back from them. Their hub carrier is meant to work with a strut which has the steering arm on it. IOW, there's no provision for the rear toe link on a Fiero.||||reg||1
